**Version:** rhai 1.25.1, features `sync`, `internals`, `metadata`; Rust stable, macOS (aarch64).

**What happens.** When a native function is not registered as a method but its first argument is a
variable, `exec_native_fn_call` swaps a copy into `args[0]` through `ArgBackup::change_first_arg_to_copy`
(`src/func/call.rs:380-385`), and restores it only on the normal-return path (`:415-417`). If the native
function panics, the unwind drops the `ArgBackup` unrestored. `Drop for ArgBackup` asserts
`orig_mut.is_none()` (`:103-110`), and a panic inside a destructor during an unwind aborts the
process (`panic_in_cleanup`). The host cannot contain it with `catch_unwind`, so one buggy native
function — or one script that provokes a panic inside rhai — takes down the whole embedding process.

**A script that triggers it with no host bug.** A closure that captures its receiver turns the receiver
into a shared cell, and method-call syntax write-locks that cell for the duration of the call
(`src/eval/target.rs:405-427`). If the native function then calls the closure with the wrong number of
arguments, building the "Function not found" message calls `Dynamic::type_id` on the captured,
write-locked cell, which does `locked_read(cell).unwrap()` (`src/types/dynamic.rs:345`). The `unwrap`
panics, and the `ArgBackup` assertion turns that panic into an abort. (With a closure of the right
arity the same capture reports a clean `ErrorDataRace`.)

**Minimal reproduction** (exits with 134 on 1.25.1):

```rust
use rhai::{Dynamic, Engine, EvalAltResult, FnPtr, NativeCallContext, Scope};

#[derive(Clone)]
struct Thing(i64);

fn with_arg(ctx: NativeCallContext, t: Thing, f: FnPtr) -> Result<Dynamic, Box<EvalAltResult>> {
    f.call_within_context(&ctx, (t,))
}

fn main() {
    let mut e = Engine::new();
    e.register_type_with_name::<Thing>("Thing");
    e.register_fn("with_arg", with_arg);
    e.register_fn("get", |t: &mut Thing| t.0);
    let mut scope = Scope::new();
    scope.push("t", Thing(7));
    // `||` takes no argument but `with_arg` passes one; `t` is captured and write-locked.
    let _ = std::panic::catch_unwind(std::panic::AssertUnwindSafe(|| {
        e.eval_with_scope::<Dynamic>(&mut scope, "t.with_arg(|| t.get())")
    }));
    println!("not reached: the process aborts with exit 134");
}
```

A native function that simply panics shows the same thing: `fn buggy(_t: Thing) -> i64 { panic!() }`,
called as `t.buggy()` or `buggy(t)`, aborts the process even inside `catch_unwind`.

**Suggested fixes:**

1. In `exec_native_fn_call`, restore the swapped first argument through the same `defer!` guard the
   script-function path already uses (`src/func/call.rs` around `:658-668`), instead of restoring only on
   the normal-return path. The guard runs on unwind too, so the assertion in `Drop for ArgBackup` can stay.
   A three-line patch against 1.25.1, which we carry, is
   https://github.com/yuvalrakavy/rhai/commit/438b8554af7bdeed0df197b8e40dc1d601d1de54 — with it every case
   above becomes an ordinary panic that `catch_unwind` contains, and the test suite passes
   (`cargo test --features sync,internals,metadata`).
2. `Dynamic::type_id` (and the type-name path used when building error messages): do not `unwrap` a
   failed `locked_read`, so a data race reports `ErrorDataRace` (as `Target::try_from` already does)
   instead of panicking.

First of all, thanks for _Rhai_, it's a really great fit for [luish](https://luish-shell.xyz/), but when I tried upgrading to 1.27.0, our test suite failed. I got `claude` to narrow it down to a change in 648c684861 and create a minimal test:

Since 1.27.0, a closure that uses a module imported at the top level of a script fails with `ErrorModuleNotFound` when the host calls it with `FnPtr::call` after the script has finished. Before 1.27.0 the closure kept the imports in scope where it was created, and the same code works on 1.26.1.

This is how a host keeps callbacks that a script registers (event handlers, completers and so on), so any such callback that calls into an imported module breaks.

## Reproduction

`Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[package]
name = "repro"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
rhai = "=1.27.0"
```

`src/main.rs`:

```rust
use rhai::module_resolvers::StaticModuleResolver;
use rhai::{Engine, FnPtr, Module, Scope};
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::Rc;

fn main() {
    let mut engine = Engine::new();

    let m = engine.compile(r#"fn who() { "from m" }"#).unwrap();
    let m = Module::eval_ast_as_new(Scope::new(), &m, &engine).unwrap();
    let mut resolver = StaticModuleResolver::new();
    resolver.insert("m", m);
    engine.set_module_resolver(resolver);

    // The host keeps a closure that the script registers, to call it later.
    let saved = Rc::new(RefCell::new(None::<FnPtr>));
    let s = saved.clone();
    engine.register_fn("keep", move |f: FnPtr| *s.borrow_mut() = Some(f));

    let ast = engine.compile(r#"import "m" as m; keep(|| m::who());"#).unwrap();
    engine.run_ast(&ast).unwrap();

    let f = saved.borrow().clone().unwrap();
    println!("{:?}", f.call::<String>(&engine, &ast, ()));
}
```

With `rhai = "=1.26.1"`:

```
Ok("from m")
```

With `rhai = "=1.27.0"`, and with `main` as of aaf90906 (2026-10-18):

```
Err(ErrorInFunctionCall("anon$08167d1babb864f0", "", ErrorModuleNotFound("m", 1:26), none))
```

It behaves the same with the default features and with `default-features = false, features = ["std", "only_i64"]`.

## Cause

Bisected to 648c684861 ("Remove unnecessary embedded environment from FnPtr", 2026-08-26): the repro prints `Ok("from m")` at `648c684861~1` and fails at `648c684861`.

That commit removed `FnPtr::env` (`Option<Shared<EncapsulatedEnviron>>`), which held the imports in scope where the closure was created. From reading the code (not verified further), the environment now comes only from `RhaiFunc::Script { env, .. }` in `global.lib`, which seems to be set only for functions compiled inside a module. A closure from the main script would then resolve `m::` against the imports of the calling `GlobalRuntimeState`, and those no longer include the script's imports once it has finished.

## Expected

As in 1.26.1: a closure keeps the imports that were in scope when it was created, wherever and whenever it is called. That is also how closures behave inside the script itself.

Fixes the abort reported in #1188. The `sync` panic behind it is split out into #1190, as requested.

`exec_native_fn_call` swaps a copy of the first argument in through `ArgBackup` when a non-method native function is called with a variable first argument, and restored it only on the normal-return path. If the native function panics, the unwind drops the `ArgBackup` unrestored, its `Drop` assertion panics during the unwind, and the process aborts, so a host's `catch_unwind` cannot contain it.

```rust
let mut engine = Engine::new();
engine.register_fn("buggy", |_x: INT| -> INT { panic!("buggy native function") });
// Aborts the process today:
let _ = std::panic::catch_unwind(std::panic::AssertUnwindSafe(|| engine.eval::<INT>("let t = 1; buggy(t)")));
```

The fix restores through the same `defer!` guard the script-function path in `func_call.rs` already uses, which also runs on unwind. The assertion is kept.

Test: `test_native_panic_with_variable_first_argument_unwinds` in `tests/native.rs`. `buggy(t)` is the aborting case and runs under every feature combination; the method-call spelling `t.buggy()` is a control and is gated on `no_object`, which disables the dot syntax (that was the CI failure on the `no_object` rows). Without the fix the test aborts with SIGABRT; with it the panic is caught and the engine is usable afterwards.

Checks: `cargo test` with default features, `cargo test --test native` under the three `no_object` rows of the CI matrix, `cargo +nightly fmt --check`. CHANGELOG line under 1.27.0's bug fixes, linking this PR.

